# packages.list — explicit package set for the Avast Omni Debian trixie slot image.
#
# Consumed by build-rootfs.sh and passed to mmdebstrap as --include=<comma list>.
# Syntax: one package per line. '#' starts a comment. Blank lines ignored.
# Recommends are OFF by default (build-rootfs.sh passes Apt::Install-Recommends "false"),
# so anything the image needs must be listed here or be a hard Depends of something listed.
#
# Base is mmdebstrap --variant=important, i.e. Essential:yes + Priority:important.
# That already gives: systemd, udev, coreutils, util-linux, procps, mount, dpkg, apt,
# bash, gzip, tar, sed, grep, findutils, login, passwd, hostname, netbase, ifupdown? (no,
# ifupdown is Priority:important on Debian but is explicitly excluded below — see NOT-INSTALLED).
#
# Size budget matters: a slot is 850 MiB (891,289,600 bytes, measured -- see
# docs/HARDWARE-MEASURED.md). Adding a package here costs space in BOTH slots.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# init / PID 1
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# systemd-sysv provides /sbin/init, reboot, poweroff, shutdown. Without it the
# kernel has no init to exec after switch_root.
systemd-sysv
# udev: DT-based interface naming (the end0-vs-eth0 problem in the plan), devtmpfs rules.
udev
# D-Bus: systemctl/networkctl/resolvectl over the bus.
dbus

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Boot chain: initramfs + the U-Boot env tools
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# initramfs-tools builds /boot/initrd.img-<ver>, which /etc/initramfs/post-update.d/99-omni-flatten
# copies onto ${mender_ramdisk_name}. The overlay-root init lives in
# /etc/initramfs-tools/scripts/local-bottom/omni-overlay.
initramfs-tools
# busybox is the shell you land in when the overlay init calls panic(). On a headless
# box in a closet this is the difference between "debuggable over serial" and "brick".
busybox
# linux-base provides linux-update-symlinks, which Armbian's linux-image postinst calls.
# apt would pull it as a dependency anyway; listed so a missing dep is a loud failure here
# rather than a surprise in the kernel deb's postinst.
linux-base
# fw_printenv / fw_setenv. ESSENTIAL: omni-arm / omni-commit / omni-rollback are pure
# fw_setenv scripts, and without these binaries a Debian slot cannot arm, commit or roll
# back — i.e. it cannot participate in A/B at all. build-rootfs.sh asserts at build time
# that both binaries exist in the finished image.
#
# It is libubootenv-tool, NOT u-boot-tools. VERIFIED against packages.debian.org for
# trixie/arm64: trixie's u-boot-tools ships only dumpimage, kwboot, mkeficapsule,
# mkenvimage, mkimage and mksunxiboot — fw_printenv/fw_setenv were split out, and
# libubootenv-tool is now their only provider in the archive.
#
# SYNTAX HAZARD (already handled in the overlay, recorded here so it stays handled):
# U-Boot 2018.09's own fw_setenv parses a `-s <file>` script as "name<space>value";
# libubootenv parses "key=value". They are not interchangeable, and a mis-parsed batch write
# lands in the single non-redundant 8 KB environment copy. /usr/lib/omni/omni-uboot-env.sh
# therefore never uses `fw_setenv -s` at all — it writes one variable per invocation, which
# is unambiguous under both implementations. Do not "optimise" that into a batched write.
libubootenv-tool

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Networking — systemd-networkd/resolved only, no ifupdown, no DHCP client package
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# In trixie systemd-resolved and systemd-timesyncd are split out of the systemd binary
# package and must be requested explicitly. systemd-networkd itself is inside `systemd`.
systemd-resolved
systemd-timesyncd
# NOTE: ifupdown is Priority:important, so --variant=important installs it. It is NOT
# deselected here with "ifupdown-": mmdebstrap marks the whole important set held, and
# apt then refuses with "Held packages were changed". It is purged in the 60-finalise
# hook instead, which is also where the forbidden-package assertion runs.
# ip / tc / bridge. tc matters because the Yocto kernel-config patch 0009 stripped the
# packet schedulers and the plan puts them back.
iproute2
# The firewall engine. On trixie `iptables` is the nft backend (iptables-nft), so anything
# that still emits iptables syntax and our native nft rules share one engine — that is the
# point of the nftables migration. iptables is kept for that compatibility surface even
# though nothing in the image requires it today.
nftables
iptables
# WireGuard userland; the module comes from Armbian's stock meson64 config (WIREGUARD=m).
wireguard-tools
# ping / arping. Debian ships these with file capabilities; note the build drops file caps
# when packing via the unshare method (see build-rootfs.sh --pack-method), which only
# affects non-root users, and this box is root-only.
iputils-ping
# Root CA bundle for curl/apt/wireguard endpoints.
ca-certificates
# curl is not optional: the streaming fallback in the flash path is
# `curl -fL "$URL" | gzip -dc | dd of=...`, so the *running* slot needs curl to write the
# other slot.
curl

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Remote access
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Host keys are deliberately removed from the image at build time; the overlay's sshd_config
# points HostKey at /data/ssh/... so identity survives an A/B flip and is not shared between
# devices. Harden SSH — but never the serial console (see the autologin drop-in).
openssh-server
# scp/ssh outbound, used by the commit-guard's positive-evidence check and for pulling
# images onto the box by hand.
openssh-client

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Storage / filesystem — the A/B flash path runs on the device
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# mke2fs (guarded mkfs.ext4 -F of the stale overlay upper), e2fsck (post-write verify of the
# inactive slot), dumpe2fs (Phase 0 inventory). The Yocto image shipped only
# e2fsprogs-mke2fs, which is why Phase 0 has to sideload e2fsck/dumpe2fs today.
e2fsprogs
# sfdisk for `sfdisk -d /dev/mmcblk0` partition-table dumps.
fdisk
# blockdev --getsize64 / --flushbufs and lsblk are in util-linux (already Essential);
# listed for intent, costs nothing.
util-linux
# mmc-utils reads EXT_CSD directly: pre_eol_info and life_time_est. A worn eMMC looks
# exactly like a DTS bug, so this needs to be answerable on the device.
mmc-utils

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Containers -- DELIBERATELY NONE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# There is no docker.io, no containerd and no runc in this image, and that is a
# decision made against the device rather than a preference.
#
# Measured on the unit 2026-08-02 (docs/HARDWARE-MEASURED.md):
#
#   * docker.service is INACTIVE and the image database holds ZERO images.
#     /var/lib/docker contains only the empty skeleton dockerd creates on its
#     first start. Nothing has ever been run.
#   * Docker cannot use the overlay2 storage driver here at all. Its data would
#     sit on the root overlayfs, and overlayfs cannot be stacked as the upperdir
#     of another overlayfs. The kernel says so out loud on every start attempt:
#         overlayfs: filesystem on '/var/lib/docker/check-overlayfs-support*/upper'
#         not supported as upperdir
#     The only fallback is the vfs driver, which stores every layer as a full
#     physical copy with no sharing -- on a 4 GB eMMC.
#   * /data (p3), the one partition that is not per-slot and therefore the only
#     sane data-root, is 150 MiB total with 130 MiB free. That does not hold one
#     image, never mind a set.
#
# So the packages were installed, never used, and could not have worked well if
# they had been. Dropping them removes ~400 MB of image, deletes the /data
# capacity problem outright, and simplifies the cgroup v2 migration (which is
# still required, but by systemd >= 256, not by Docker's cgroup driver).
#
# TO PUT IT BACK you need three things, in this order, and the first is not
# optional: capacity, then a storage driver, then the packages.
#   1. Repartition. p5/p6 are 650 MiB each and hold 2.0 MB of actual data; that
#      is where the space is. This rewrites the partition table on a device whose
#      p7 holds a factory image this repo cannot rebuild, so it belongs behind a
#      full backup.
#   2. Give Docker a data-root on a REAL filesystem, not on the overlay -- e.g.
#      /data on the enlarged p3 -- so overlay2 works.
#   3. Add `docker.io` back here plus /etc/docker/daemon.json with
#      { "data-root": ..., "exec-opts": ["native.cgroupdriver=systemd"] }
#      and containerd's root= pointed alongside it. Both files are in git history.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Phase 4 gate + on-device debugging
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ethtool: link speed/duplex assertion and `ethtool -S` rx CRC/length error counters —
# the per-direction RGMII delay check depends on it.
ethtool
# iperf3: the 30-minute bidirectional throughput comparison, 5.4 vs 6.12, each direction
# recorded separately.
iperf3
# fio: the 2 h `--direct=1 --verify=crc32c --verify_fatal=1` eMMC verification run.
fio
# tcpdump: packet-level truth when the NIC "mostly works". Small; keep it.
tcpdump
# less/jq: reading long serial output without a pager is miserable, and the arm/commit
# scripts parse JSON status.
less
jq

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DELIBERATELY NOT INSTALLED — build-rootfs.sh asserts these are absent
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   flash-kernel      Recommended by Debian's arm64 linux-image. It writes to the boot
#                     device on kernel install. This device's kernel is loaded from inside
#                     the rootfs slot by Mender bootcmd; flash-kernel would be actively
#                     destructive.
#   u-boot-menu       Same class of problem: rewrites boot configuration.
#   armbian-bsp-cli   Pulls armbian-install, which REPARTITIONS eMMC and rewrites
#   armbian-config    bootloaders. This is the single reason the image is built with
#   armbian-firmware  mmdebstrap instead of Armbian's image pipeline.
#   ifupdown          Replaced by systemd-networkd; having both is a split-brain network.
#   isc-dhcp-client   networkd has its own DHCP client.
#   network-manager   Ditto, and it fights networkd over the same link.
#   resolvconf        systemd-resolved owns /etc/resolv.conf.
#   openresolv
#   rsyslog           journald with Storage=volatile (set in the overlay) is enough; a
#                     disk-backed syslog writes into the per-slot overlay upper forever.
